{title:Title}

{phang}
{bf:creshape} {hline 2} C-accelerated conversion of data from wide to long form and back


{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{pstd}
Basic syntax

{phang2}
Convert data from wide form to long form

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:long} {it:stubnames}{cmd:,} {opth i(varlist)} [{it:options}]

{phang2}
Convert data from long form to wide form

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:wide} {it:stubnames}{cmd:,} {opth i(varlist)} [{it:options}]

{phang2}
Convert data back to long form after using {cmd:creshape wide} (or {cmd:reshape wide})

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:long} [{cmd:,} {opt v:erbose} {opt thr:eads(#)}]

{phang2}
Convert data back to wide form after using {cmd:creshape long} (or {cmd:reshape long})

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:wide} [{cmd:,} {opt v:erbose} {opt thr:eads(#)}]

{phang2}
List problem observations when {cmd:creshape} fails

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:error}

{pstd}
Advanced syntax

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:i} {varlist}

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:j} {varname} [{it:values}] [{cmd:,} {opt s:tring}]

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:xij} {it:fvarnames} [{cmd:,} {cmd:atwl(}{it:chars}{cmd:)}]

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:xi} [{varlist}]

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} [{cmdab:q:uery}]

{p 12 16 2}
{cmd:creshape} {cmd:clear}

{synoptset 28 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{syntab:As in reshape}
{p2coldent:* {opth i(varlist)}}use {it:varlist} as the ID variables{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:j(}{varname} [{it:values}]{cmd:)}}long to wide: {it:varname}, an existing variable;
wide to long: {it:varname}, a new variable; optionally the values to use{p_end}
{synopt:{opt s:tring}}{it:varname} is a string variable (default is numeric){p_end}
{synopt:{cmdab:at:wl(}{it:chars}{cmd:)}}use {it:chars} for {cmd:@} in the names of the long form{p_end}
{synopt:{opt fav:or(favor)}}accepted for compatibility with {cmd:reshape}; recorded, not used{p_end}

{syntab:ctools}
{synopt:{opt v:erbose}}display timing breakdown{p_end}
{synopt:{opt thr:eads(#)}}maximum number of threads to use{p_end}
{synoptline}
{p 4 6 2}* {opt i(varlist)} is required.{p_end}

{pstd}
where {it:values} is {it:#}[{cmd:-}{it:#}] [...] if the {cmd:j} variable is
numeric (the default), or {it:string} [...] if it is string; and
{it:stubnames} and {it:fvarnames} are variable names (long to wide), or stubs
of variable names (wide to long), either of which may contain {cmd:@},
denoting where {cmd:j} appears or is to appear in the names of the wide form.


{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:creshape} reimplements Stata's {cmd:reshape} ({cmd:reshape.ado} 4.7.1,
October 4, 2023). See {help creshape##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for
details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:creshape} converts data from wide to long form and vice versa.

            {it:long}
           {c TLC}{hline 15}{c TRC}                  {it:wide}
           {c |} {it:i  j}  {it:stub} {c |}                 {c TLC}{hline 18}{c TRC}
           {c |}{hline 15}{c |}                 {c |} {it:i}  {it:stub}{bf:1} {it:stub}{bf:2} {c |}
           {c |} 1  {bf:1}   4.1 {c |}     creshape    {c |}{hline 18}{c |}
           {c |} 1  {bf:2}   4.5 {c |}   <{hline 9}>   {c |} 1    4.1   4.5 {c |}
           {c |} 2  {bf:1}   3.3 {c |}                 {c |} 2    3.3   3.0 {c |}
           {c |} 2  {bf:2}   3.0 {c |}                 {c BLC}{hline 18}{c BRC}
           {c BLC}{hline 15}{c BRC}

{pstd}
It takes the syntax of {help reshape} and gives the same data: the same
observations in the same order, the same variables with the same storage
types, display formats, labels, notes and characteristics, and the same
settings recorded with the data, so {cmd:reshape} and {cmd:creshape} can be
mixed freely on one dataset. The observations are moved by the ctools plugin,
on several threads, which makes {cmd:creshape} many times faster than
{cmd:reshape} on large data.


{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{phang}
{opth i(varlist)} specifies the variables whose unique values denote a
logical observation. {opt i()} is required.

{phang}
{cmd:j(}{varname} [{it:values}]{cmd:)} specifies the variable whose unique
values denote a subobservation. {it:values} lists the values to be used from
{it:varname}; they are usually left out, because {cmd:creshape} finds them
in the data ({cmd:creshape wide}) or in the variable names
({cmd:creshape long}). Without {opt j()}, the variable is named {cmd:_j}.

{phang}
{opt string} specifies that {cmd:j()} may contain string values.

{phang}
{cmd:atwl(}{it:chars}{cmd:)} specifies that {it:chars} be substituted for the
{cmd:@} character when the data are converted from wide to long form.
{cmd:reshape} accepts {cmd:atwl()} only in its advanced syntax
({cmd:reshape xij} ...{cmd:, atwl()}); {cmd:creshape} accepts it in the basic
syntax too.

{phang}
{opt favor(favor)}, with {it:favor} one of {cmd:speed} or {cmd:memory}, is
accepted so that {cmd:reshape} commands run unchanged. The value is recorded
in {cmd:_dta[ReS_favor]}, where a later {cmd:reshape} finds it;
{cmd:creshape} has one method and does not use it.

{phang}
{opt verbose} displays the time spent in each step: copying the data into the
plugin, ordering and checking them, describing the result, and writing it.

{phang}
{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256. Omitting
it or specifying zero uses the {help ctools##threads:ctools setting}.


{marker remarks}{...}
{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
{bf:What is checked.} As {cmd:reshape} does, {cmd:creshape} refuses a request
that would lose information, with the same messages and return codes: in the
wide form the {cmd:i} variables must identify the observations; in the long
form {cmd:i} and {cmd:j} together must, {cmd:j} may not be missing, and every
variable that is not reshaped must be constant within {cmd:i}. These checks
run before the data are changed. {cmd:creshape error} then lists the
problem observations.

{pstd}
{bf:Settings.} The names given in the basic syntax are recorded as
characteristics of the dataset ({cmd:_dta[ReS_i]}, {cmd:_dta[ReS_j]},
{cmd:_dta[ReS_Xij]} and the others listed under
{help creshape##results:Stored results}). They are what makes
{cmd:creshape long} and {cmd:creshape wide} work without arguments, and they
are the same characteristics {cmd:reshape} uses. The subcommands that only
declare, show or clear these settings ({cmd:creshape i}, {cmd:j}, {cmd:xij},
{cmd:xi}, {cmd:query}, {cmd:clear}), and {cmd:creshape error}, are carried out
by {cmd:reshape} itself.

{pstd}
{bf:How the data are moved.} The plugin copies every observation into its own
memory, checks the request there, and orders the observations by {cmd:i}
(data that are already in order are not sorted again). Stata then saves the
dataset without observations to a temporary file; the plugin reads that
description (storage types, formats, labels, notes, characteristics and
value labels), rewrites it for the new form, and Stata loads it and sets the
number of observations. Finally the plugin writes the values, a block of
observations at a time on each thread. The result is marked as sorted by
{cmd:i} (and {cmd:j} in long form), as {cmd:reshape} leaves it.

{pstd}
{bf:Memory.} While it runs, {cmd:creshape} holds the original data in the
plugin (8 bytes for each numeric value, the declared width plus one for each
string) next to the reshaped data in Stata.

{pstd}
{bf:If something fails.} Between the check and the end of the command the
plugin holds the only copy of the observations, so {cmd:creshape} does not
respond to Break during that time. If Stata cannot build the new form (for
lack of memory, say), the original data are put back exactly: values, order,
sort order, labels and characteristics.

{pstd}
{bf:strL variables.} A plugin cannot write {cmd:strL} values. When the
dataset holds a {cmd:strL} variable, a {cmd:str2045} variable, or a string
variable whose values would take more than 2 GB, {cmd:creshape} runs
{cmd:reshape} itself; the result is the same and only the speed is lost.
{cmd:verbose} says when this happens.

{pstd}
{bf:Differences from reshape.} They are few and deliberate:

{phang2}
o  A new {cmd:j} variable is stored in the smallest type that holds its
values; {cmd:reshape} with {cmd:favor(speed)} stores values beyond 2,147,483,620
as missing.

{phang2}
o  With values listed in {cmd:j()}, {cmd:reshape wide} with
{cmd:favor(speed)} reports "no data" for every value listed after one that
has no observations, and leaves out their variables. {cmd:creshape} leaves
out only the values that have no observations.

{phang2}
o  When a request is refused, {cmd:reshape} leaves the data sorted by
{cmd:i}; {cmd:creshape} leaves them as they were.

{phang2}
o  Value labels that are not attached to any variable are kept;
{cmd:reshape} drops them.

{phang2}
o  The syntax of Stata 10 and earlier ({cmd:reshape groups}, {cmd:vars},
{cmd:cons}) is passed to {cmd:reshape}, as are data whose settings were
declared in that syntax.


{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{pstd}Wide to long, and back{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse reshape1, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. creshape long inc ue, i(id) j(year)}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. creshape wide}{p_end}

{pstd}Long to wide{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse reshape6, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. creshape wide inc ue, i(id) j(year)}{p_end}

{pstd}String values of {cmd:j}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse reshape4, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. creshape long inc, i(id) j(sex) string}{p_end}

{pstd}{cmd:j} in the middle of the variable names{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse reshape3, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. creshape long inc@r ue, i(id) j(year)}{p_end}

{pstd}Timings and the number of threads{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. creshape wide, verbose threads(4)}{p_end}


{marker results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:creshape long} and {cmd:creshape wide} store the following in {cmd:r()}:

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 24 28 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N)}}number of observations in the result{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(k)}}number of variables in the result{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(N_before)}}number of observations before{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(k_before)}}number of variables before{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(time_load)}}seconds copying the data into the plugin{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(time_plan)}}seconds ordering and checking the data{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(time_schema)}}seconds describing the result{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(time_store)}}seconds writing the result{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(threads_max)}}maximum number of threads{p_end}

{p2col 5 24 28 2: Macros}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(cmd)}}{cmd:creshape}{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
Like {cmd:reshape}, {cmd:creshape} stores the following characteristics with
the data (see {manhelp char P}):

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_i]}}{cmd:i} variable names{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_j]}}{cmd:j} variable name{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_jv]}}{cmd:j} values, if specified{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_Xij]}}{cmd:X_ij} variable names{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_Xij_n]}}number of {cmd:X_ij} variables{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_Xij_long}{it:#}{cmd:]}}name of {it:#}th {cmd:X_ij} variable in long form{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_Xij_wide}{it:#}{cmd:]}}names of {it:#}th {cmd:X_ij} variable in wide form{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[Res_Xi]}}{cmd:X_i} variable names, if specified{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_atwl]}}{cmd:atwl()} value, if specified{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_str]}}{cmd:1} if option {cmd:string} specified, {cmd:0} otherwise{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_dta[ReS_favor]}}{cmd:favor()} value, if specified{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
After {cmd:creshape wide}, the variable label and value label of {cmd:j} and
the variable labels of the long variables are kept in
{cmd:_dta[__JVarLab]}, {cmd:_dta[__JValLabName]}, {cmd:_dta[__JValLab]} and
{cmd:_dta[__XijVarLab}{it:name}{cmd:]}, from which {cmd:creshape long} (or
{cmd:reshape long}) restores them.


{* ctools-attribution:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{marker acknowledgments}{...}
{title:Acknowledgments}

{pstd}
{cmd:creshape} reimplements {cmd:reshape}, an official Stata command. Its
syntax, options, messages, summary table, and the characteristics it records
with the data follow {cmd:reshape}. The subcommands that only declare, show or
clear the settings ({cmd:creshape i}, {cmd:j}, {cmd:xij}, {cmd:xi},
{cmd:query}, {cmd:clear}), and {cmd:creshape error}, are carried out by
{cmd:reshape} itself.

{pstd}
{cmd:creshape} is pinned to this version of {cmd:reshape}:

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:reshape} (official Stata){p_end}
{p2col:Authors}StataCorp LLC{p_end}
{p2col:Version}{cmd:reshape.ado} 4.7.1{p_end}
{p2col:Date}October 4, 2023{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! version 4.7.1  04oct2023}{p_end}
{p2col:Shipped with}StataNow 19.5 (revision 14 Oct 2026){p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:creshape} follows {cmd:reshape} as it behaves in this Stata release.
{cmd:which reshape} shows the version of {cmd:reshape.ado} in your Stata.
{help ctools##acknowledgments:help ctools} lists the pinned versions of all
ctools commands.

{pstd}
Please report bugs and questions about {cmd:creshape} at
{browse "https://github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues":github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues}.
StataCorp does not support {cmd:creshape}. Undocumented differences between
{cmd:creshape} and {cmd:reshape} are bugs in {cmd:creshape}.

{pstd}
The Stata manual acknowledges that this version of {cmd:reshape} was based in
part on the work of Jeroen Weesie of Utrecht University (see
{bf:[D] reshape}).

{pstd}
The original command is described in:

{phang2}Weesie, J. 1997. dm48: An enhancement of reshape.
{it:Stata Technical Bulletin} 38: 2-4. Reprinted in
{it:Stata Technical Bulletin Reprints}, vol. 7, pp. 40-43. College Station,
TX: Stata Press.{p_end}
